# Escape by default

### The html template and SafeHtml

Week 3, Rung 2 of 7: Lite. Talk 2

---

## The problem

Every page holds text the generator did not write: titles, summaries, step names, URLs, Markdown bodies.

```
# Arrays <and> objects        <- a title
A "quick" look at 'quotes'     <- a summary
```

Put that text into HTML as it is, and the browser reads `<and>` as a tag and a `"` as the end of an
attribute. With a little more effort, an author (or anyone who can edit a course) puts a script on every
reader's page. This is cross-site scripting.

The fix is old and simple: **escape** text before it becomes HTML. The hard part is never forgetting.

---

## Five characters

```ts
// variants/rung-2-lite/src/html.ts
const ENTITIES: Record<string, string> = { "&": "&amp;", "<": "&lt;", ">": "&gt;", '"': "&quot;", "'": "&#39;" };

/** Text as HTML: safe in element content and in quoted attribute values. */
export function escapeHtml(text: string): string {
  return text.replace(/[&<>"']/g, (char) => ENTITIES[char]!);
}
```

| Character | Why |
| --- | --- |
| `<`, `>` | would start or end a tag |
| `&` | would start an entity; escape it first so nothing is escaped twice |
| `"`, `'` | would end a quoted attribute value |

Safe in element content and in **quoted** attributes. Every attribute in rung 2 is quoted.

---

## Forgetting is the bug

```ts
const card = `<h3><a href="${dir}index.html">${lo.title}</a></h3>`;            // forgot
const card = `<h3><a href="${dir}index.html">${escapeHtml(lo.title)}</a></h3>`; // remembered, this time
```

String concatenation makes the safe form the one you have to remember. Fifty templates later, one is
forgotten, and no test notices until someone writes a title with a `<` in it.

Make the safe form the default, and the unsafe form the one you have to ask for.

---

## The Auto-escaping Template

```ts
// variants/rung-2-lite/src/html.ts
export class SafeHtml {
  readonly value: string;
  constructor(value: string) { this.value = value; }
}

type Value = string | number | SafeHtml | null | undefined | false | readonly Value[];

function render(value: Value): string {
  if (value instanceof SafeHtml) return value.value;          // already HTML: keep it
  if (Array.isArray(value)) return value.map(render).join("");
  if (value === null || value === undefined || value === false) return "";
  return escapeHtml(String(value));                           // anything else: escape it
}

/** A tagged template: html`<h1>${title}</h1>` escapes `title` unless it is SafeHtml. */
export function html(strings: TemplateStringsArray, ...values: Value[]): SafeHtml {
  return new SafeHtml(strings.reduce((out, string, i) => out + render(values[i - 1]) + string));
}
```

---

## How a tagged template works

```ts
html`<h1>${title}</h1>`
// calls html(["<h1>", "</h1>"], title)
```

The **literal** parts (`strings`) are written by the programmer, so they are trusted. The **values** are
data, so each is escaped, unless it is already `SafeHtml`.

```ts
html`<h1>${"<b>x</b>"}</h1>`.value          // "<h1>&lt;b&gt;x&lt;/b&gt;</h1>"
html`<p>${html`<b>${"&"}</b>`}</p>`.value   // "<p><b>&amp;</b></p>"   nested: kept, inner value escaped
html`<ul>${["<a>", html`<li>b</li>`]}</ul>` // arrays: each item rendered by the same rule
html`${null}${undefined}${false}${0}`       // "0": absent values vanish, so `cond && html`...`` works
```

Returning `SafeHtml`, not `string`, is what lets templates nest without escaping twice.

---

## The type does the remembering

```ts
function card(lo: Lo, dir: string): SafeHtml {
  ...
  return html`<li class="card">${kind}<h3><a href="${dir}index.html">${lo.title}</a></h3><p>${lo.summary}</p></li>`;
}
```

`lo.title` is a `string`, so it is escaped. `kind` is `SafeHtml`, so it is kept. The programmer writes the
template the obvious way and gets the safe result.

`renderMarkdown` returns `SafeHtml`, because it escapes its input before it adds tags (note "A Markdown subset"). So a page
body can be passed straight into `page(...)`.

---

## The one way around it

`new SafeHtml(text)` trusts `text` completely. Used on course text, it undoes everything.

So rung 2 has a rule, kept as a test (L3) with a teaching message (L4):

```ts
// variants/rung-2-lite/tests/html.test.ts
test("only html.ts and markdown.ts make SafeHtml, because only they escape what they wrap", () => {
  const makers = readdirSync(src).filter((file) => /new SafeHtml\(/.test(readFileSync(join(src, file), "utf8")));
  assert.deepEqual(makers.sort(), ["html.ts", "markdown.ts"],
    "Trusted HTML rule: `new SafeHtml(...)` skips escaping, so it may appear only in src/html.ts and src/markdown.ts. " +
    "Build the HTML with the html`` template instead; it escapes every value for you.");
});
```

And `AGENTS.md` puts "adding a file that makes `SafeHtml`" behind the Human Boundary.

---

## Escaping is tested on whole pages

```ts
// variants/rung-2-lite/tests/site-builder.test.ts
test("course text never reaches a page unescaped", () => {
  const evil = "<script>alert(1)</script>";
  const course: Course = {
    type: "course", id: "c", title: evil, summary: evil, contentMd: `# ${evil}\n\n${evil}`, route: "/",
    los: [{ type: "topic", ..., los: [
      { type: "note", ..., title: evil, summary: evil, contentMd: evil },
      { type: "web", ..., route: `"><script>alert(1)</script>` },
      { type: "archive", ..., archiveFile: evil },
      { type: "lab", ..., los: [{ type: "step", ..., title: evil, contentMd: evil }] },
    ] }],
  };
  for (const page of buildSite(course)) assert.doesNotMatch(page.html, /<script/, `${page.path} contains an unescaped <script>`);
});
```

Every field of every kind, on every page. `AGENTS.md`: every new piece of course text gets a case here.

---

## The same note, two generators

A note in a copy of the fixture ends with a script tag and a `<b>bold</b>` tag. Built by each:

| | Upstream `tutors website` | Rung 2 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| the script tag | in the page, as a script | `&lt;script&gt;...`, shown as text |
| the bold tag | bold | `&lt;b&gt;bold&lt;/b&gt;`, shown as text |

Why upstream differs: markdown-it runs with `html: true`, and the Vento engine is set up with
`autoescape: false` (`packages/jsr/gen/src/templates/template-engine.ts`). Raw HTML is a feature for
authors; the reader (rung 5) sanitises what it shows (rung 6).

You will build both in the lab and look.

---

## What escaping does not cover

Escaping stops new tags and broken attributes. It does not judge a **URL**:

```ts
renderInline("[click](javascript:document.body.remove%28%29)")
// '<a href="javascript:document.body.remove%28%29">click</a>'   escaped, valid, and still runs when clicked
```

That needs an allow-list of URL schemes, which is sanitising, not escaping. `RUNG.md` names the gap, and
rung 6 (Guarded) closes it. A fence that refuses a sanitiser library on this rung names that rung too.

---

## The fence for a static site

```ts
// variants/rung-2-lite/tests/rung-fence.test.ts
{ pattern: /<script|\bon(click|load|error)=/i, what: "client-side JavaScript",
  why: "Rung 2 writes static pages. Interactive pages arrive with Svelte in rung 5 (Reader)." },
```

Rung 1's fence refused HTML; rung 2's allows it and refuses script instead. That one changed rule is the
Rung Delta of the fence. No dependency is still the rule: TypeScript as a dev dependency arrives in rung 3 (Layers), test
libraries in rung 4 (Proven), URL sanitising in rung 6 (Guarded), and a full Markdown library only at the Summit.

---

## Summary

- escape five characters, always, before text becomes HTML
- the `html` tagged template escapes every value unless it is `SafeHtml`
- only `html.ts` and `markdown.ts` may make `SafeHtml`, and a test says so
- escaping is tested on every page, for every field of every kind
- URLs and raw HTML for authors are sanitising problems: rung 6

Read the note "Escaping by construction", then do Lab 3.
